"""
Confines the running process to what a policy allows, and says what really confines it.

- A policy names promises in the words of pledge, and paths in the permission letters of unveil.
- On OpenBSD the paths are unveiled, the view locked and the promises pledged, with execpromises.
- A list of paths unveil has no room for applies nothing at all.
- With allowFolding siblings are folded onto their parent and tried again; that widens the view.
- On Linux no new privileges is set and the paths become a Landlock ruleset.
- On Linux a seccomp filter refuses the calls a confined programme has no use for.
- On Linux the promises are checked and not enforced.
- On macOS the programme is started again under a Seatbelt profile of the paths, from its first line.
- On macOS everything before applyPolicy runs twice, the second time confined: apply it first.
- On macOS the promises allow the network, fork and exec as a whole; the rest are not enforced.
- On FreeBSD the paths are opened with their rights and the process enters capability mode.
- On FreeBSD that needs promises without network or exec; with others or none nothing is applied.
- On FreeBSD a path the runtime does not open itself, such as a database's file, is not reachable.
- On Android, on any other platform and in a library nothing is applied: not available.
- The report names each mechanism that took effect, and no other.
- Nothing here stops the programme: what an unconfined run means is the programme's decision.

    let promised: Policy := allowPromises(makePolicy(), "stdio rpath inet");
    let policy: Policy := allowPath(promised, "/var/www", "r");
    let report: Report := applyPolicy(policy);
    printLine(describeReport(report));
"""
pragma Targets(Names => "linux openbsd freebsd darwin");
import Standard.String (
    String
);

module Standard.Sandbox is
    """
    One way of confining the process.
    """
    type Mechanism: Free;

    """
    No new privileges on Linux: no execve of this process or its children
    gains privilege.
    """
    constant mechanismNoNewPrivileges: Mechanism;

    """
    pledge on OpenBSD: the process may only make the calls its promises
    allow, and is stopped at any other.
    """
    constant mechanismPledge: Mechanism;

    """
    unveil on OpenBSD: the process sees only the paths of the policy, and
    the view is locked.
    """
    constant mechanismUnveil: Mechanism;

    """
    Landlock on Linux: the process reaches only the paths of the policy,
    with their permissions.
    """
    constant mechanismLandlock: Mechanism;

    """
    The seccomp filter on Linux: tracing another process, loading a kernel
    module, mounting, rebooting and the like fail as not permitted.
    """
    constant mechanismSeccomp: Mechanism;

    """
    execpromises on OpenBSD: a programme this process starts runs under
    the execpromises and inside the unveiled view.
    """
    constant mechanismExecPromises: Mechanism;

    """
    Seatbelt on macOS: the process reaches only the paths of the policy,
    the system's own baseline and its libraries, and without the
    promises inet, unix or dns no network, without proc no fork, and
    without exec no programme but itself.  A programme it starts stays
    under the same profile.
    """
    constant mechanismSeatbelt: Mechanism;

    """
    Capsicum on FreeBSD: the process is in capability mode and reaches only
    the paths of the policy, each with the rights of its permissions, and
    no network address and no programme to start.
    """
    constant mechanismCapsicum: Mechanism;

    """
    Whether the process can be confined here: true on OpenBSD, on Linux, on
    macOS and on FreeBSD, false on Android, on any other platform and in a
    library.  Where it is
    false, applyPolicy applies nothing and describeReport says not
    available.  A library would confine the whole process of its host, and
    Android's own filter ends an app at a call it does not allow.
    No contract: the answer is a Bool, nothing cheaper holds.
    """
    function sandboxAvailable(): Bool;

    """
    What a process is allowed, collected before it is applied.
    """
    type Policy: Linear;

    """
    A policy that allows no path and names no promise.
    No contract: a value of its own type built from its arguments; no property cheaper than its type holds.
    """
    function makePolicy(): Policy;

    """
    Adds promises: words of pledge separated by one space each, such as
    stdio rpath.  A word pledge does not know, an empty word or an empty
    list makes the policy invalid.  Without this call nothing is pledged.
    No contract: a value of its own type built from its arguments; no property cheaper than its type holds.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function allowPromises(policy: Policy, promises: Span[Nat8, R]): Policy;

    """
    Adds execpromises, checked as allowPromises checks promises: the words a
    programme this process starts runs under.  On OpenBSD such a programme
    then keeps them and the unveiled view; without this call it runs
    without pledge and without the view.  Elsewhere they are checked and
    not enforced.
    No contract: a value of its own type built from its arguments; no property cheaper than its type holds.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function allowExecPromises(policy: Policy, promises: Span[Nat8, R]): Policy;

    """
    Adds a path with permissions: some of r (read), w (write), x (execute)
    and c (create), each at most once.  An empty path, a path with a zero
    byte, or other permissions make the policy invalid.
    No contract: a value of its own type built from its arguments; no property cheaper than its type holds.
    """
    generic [R: Region, S: Region]
    function allowPath(policy: Policy, path: Span[Nat8, R], permissions: Span[Nat8, S]): Policy;

    """
    Lets applyPolicy fold the paths on OpenBSD when unveil has no room for
    them: siblings are folded as foldPolicy does, down to 128 paths, and
    the room is measured again.  Without this call a list unveil has no
    room for applies nothing.
    No contract: a value of its own type built from its arguments; no property cheaper than its type holds.
    """
    function allowFolding(policy: Policy): Policy;

    """
    Folds paths until at most target are left or nothing more folds.  Each
    step replaces the largest group of paths that share a parent directory
    and the same permissions by that parent with those permissions.  A
    group is never folded onto the root, nor onto a parent the policy
    grants more than the group.  The parent grants its whole directory,
    so folding widens what the process reaches.
    No contract: a value of its own type built from its arguments; no property cheaper than its type holds.
    """
    function foldPolicy(policy: Policy, target: Index): Policy;

    """
    The number of paths in the policy.
    No contract: a number read from its argument; its type gives its range, and nothing cheaper holds.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function policyPathCount(policy: &[Policy, R]): Index;

    """
    The paths of the policy, one per line, each followed by a space and its
    permissions.
    No contract: a text whose length follows its input.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function describePaths(policy: &[Policy, R]): String;

    """
    Whether nothing so far has made the policy invalid.
    No contract: the answer is a Bool, nothing cheaper holds.
    """
    generic [R: Region]
    function policyValid(policy: &[Policy, R]): Bool;

    """
    Gives the policy back without applying it.
    No contract: it only frees or closes what it is given.
    """
    function discardPolicy(policy: Policy): Unit;

    """
    What applyPolicy did.
    """
    type Report: Free;

    """
    Applies the policy.  An invalid policy applies nothing, and on OpenBSD
    neither does a list of paths unveil has no room for.  A policy
    without paths lets the process reach no file.  On Linux Landlock
    confines the threads started after it, so it is not applied while other
    threads run: apply the policy before the first task.  On macOS the
    programme starts again from its first line under the profile, the
    first process waits for it and ends as it ends, so this call returns
    only in the confined process, or where it could not start, unconfined.
    It is not tried while other threads run.
    No contract: a value of its own type built from its arguments; no property cheaper than its type holds.
    """
    function applyPolicy(policy: Policy): Report;

    """
    Whether the policy was invalid, so that nothing was tried.
    No contract: the answer is a Bool, nothing cheaper holds.
    """
    function reportRefused(report: Report): Bool;

    """
    Whether any mechanism took effect.
    No contract: the answer is a Bool, nothing cheaper holds.
    """
    function reportApplied(report: Report): Bool;

    """
    Whether the mechanism took effect.
    No contract: the answer is a Bool, nothing cheaper holds.
    """
    function reportHas(report: Report, mechanism: Mechanism): Bool;

    """
    The number of paths in the policy.
    No contract: a number read from its argument; its type gives its range, and nothing cheaper holds.
    """
    function reportPathsAsked(report: Report): Index;

    """
    The number of paths unveil, Landlock, Seatbelt or Capsicum took.  A
    path that was not taken is not reachable: a failure narrows the view,
    it never widens it.
    No contract: a number read from its argument; its type gives its range, and nothing cheaper holds.
    """
    function reportPathsApplied(report: Report): Index;

    """
    How many paths folding removed before the list fitted; 0 when nothing
    was folded.
    No contract: a number read from its argument; its type gives its range, and nothing cheaper holds.
    """
    function reportPathsFolded(report: Report): Index;

    """
    Whether unveil had no room for the paths, even after folding where the
    policy allowed it, so that nothing was applied.
    No contract: the answer is a Bool, nothing cheaper holds.
    """
    function reportBudgetExceeded(report: Report): Bool;

    """
    The number of the first path unveil had no room for, counted from 1 in
    the list as it was measured, after any folding; 0 when it had room.
    No contract: a number read from its argument; its type gives its range, and nothing cheaper holds.
    """
    function reportFirstRefused(report: Report): Index;

    """
    The version of Landlock the kernel offers, 0 when it has none, -1 on a
    platform without Landlock.
    No contract: a number read from its argument; its type gives its range, and nothing cheaper holds.
    """
    function reportLandlockAbi(report: Report): Int32;

    """
    Whether Landlock or Seatbelt was not applied because other threads were
    running.
    No contract: the answer is a Bool, nothing cheaper holds.
    """
    function reportThreadsInTheWay(report: Report): Bool;

    """
    Whether Capsicum was not applied because the promises were missing or
    need the network or exec, which capability mode forbids.
    No contract: the answer is a Bool, nothing cheaper holds.
    """
    function reportCapsicumRefused(report: Report): Bool;

    """
    A line in the form of the sandbox output of the command-line standard,
    such as sandbox: pledge, unveil 2 of 2 paths, or on macOS sandbox:
    seatbelt 2 of 2 paths.
    No contract: a text whose length follows its input.
    """
    function describeReport(report: Report): String;

    """
    Whether no new privileges is set, as the kernel answers; None where it
    cannot be asked.
    No contract: an Option result, which an ensures cannot look into.
    """
    function queryNoNewPrivileges(): Option[Bool];

    """
    Whether the process is in capability mode, as the kernel answers; None
    where it cannot be asked.
    No contract: an Option result, which an ensures cannot look into.
    """
    function queryCapsicum(): Option[Bool];

    """
    Whether a call the seccomp filter refuses is refused: the process reads
    one byte of its own memory through process_vm_readv, which needs no
    privilege.  None where it cannot be asked.
    No contract: an Option result, which an ensures cannot look into.
    """
    function querySeccomp(): Option[Bool];

    """
    Whether the kernel confirms every mechanism the report claims that it
    can be asked about: no new privileges, the seccomp filter and
    capability mode.  A
    mechanism the report does not claim may still be in force, set by the
    parent process or a container, and is not held against it.  pledge,
    unveil, Landlock and Seatbelt cannot be asked; a forbidden call shows
    them.
    No contract: the answer is a Bool, nothing cheaper holds.
    """
    function reportAgreesWithSystem(report: Report): Bool;
end module.
